---
term: Charter formation
section: life-cycle
order: 10
definition: The creation of a new charter and the corporation that it constitutes.
seeAlso:
  - independent-formation
  - admission
  - conversion
  - dissolution
---
A charter can form in three ways:

- **Ex natura.** A charter forms without a parent and exists directly under the [[Natural Charter]]. See [[independent formation]].
- **Into a parent.** A charter joins an existing [[parent corporation]] somewhere along a gradient. At one end, [[voluntary admission]], autonomously organized struggle establishes the charter where it stands, and the charter then seeks admission. At the other end, [[commission]], the parent organizes a subsidiary charter to establish itself on a jurisdiction.
- **As a new parent.** Several parties convert a [[contract]] among them into a charter with its own organization. See [[conversion]].

Most cases that join a parent fall between the two ends of the gradient. A [[contract]] between the charter and the parent sets the exact composition and terms of each case. The charter is an [[associate]] of the parent while the contract lasts, and becomes a member at [[admission]].

The parent can be national, territorial, economic, organizational, functional, or any other relevant corporation.
